1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,659
At the Corner Cafe, Asha now has three
programs.

2
00:00:03,679 --> 00:00:06,660
And each one has its own copy of with tax.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,869
Chapter 17 ended with exactly this
problem.

4
00:00:09,889 --> 00:00:14,318
If she finds a bug in with tax, she must
fix it three times.

5
00:00:14,338 --> 00:00:17,358
Miss one copy, and two programs disagree.

6
00:00:17,953 --> 00:00:23,563
This is Code Quest, Chapter 18: write your
tools once, and import them everywhere.

7
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:28,545
And an AI assistant will recommend a
ready-made function, and even name the

8
00:00:28,565 --> 00:00:29,870
page where it lives.

9
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:30,995
Will it be right?

10
00:00:31,015 --> 00:00:33,246
You will check that page yourself.

11
00:00:33,853 --> 00:00:37,033
Here is today's mission: Asha's day
report.

12
00:00:37,053 --> 00:00:39,043
It has no copy of with tax.

13
00:00:39,063 --> 00:00:44,103
Lines 2 to 5 import it, together with
three of Python's own modules.

14
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:50,395
Lines 13 to 16 print a total, a biscuit
order, a closing time and a file name.

15
00:00:50,415 --> 00:00:51,603
Let's build it.

16
00:00:52,183 --> 00:00:57,981
Your goals: import modules in three ways,
write your own module, use three standard

17
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:03,448
modules, check an AI's suggestion in the
documentation, and fix import errors.

18
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:08,490
A module is a Python file whose functions
other programs can use.

19
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:12,950
Write with tax once, fix it once, and
every program gets the fix.

20
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,326
Python also comes with a large set of
modules, already written and tested.

21
00:01:18,346 --> 00:01:20,783
It is called the standard library.

22
00:01:20,803 --> 00:01:23,239
You met one in Chapter 10: random.

23
00:01:23,817 --> 00:01:27,857
Importing makes a module's functions
available in your program.

24
00:01:27,877 --> 00:01:32,497
Python finds the module file, runs it
once, and gives you a name for it.

25
00:01:33,087 --> 00:01:35,707
There are three common ways to import.

26
00:01:35,727 --> 00:01:39,737
Import math keeps the module name in
front: math dot ceil.

27
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:44,817
From math import ceil brings only that one
function in, so you call it on its own.

28
00:01:44,867 --> 00:01:48,751
And import math as m gives the module a
short name.

29
00:01:48,771 --> 00:01:51,966
A short name like this is called an alias.

30
00:01:52,587 --> 00:01:55,319
Think of a module as a toolbox on a shelf.

31
00:01:55,339 --> 00:01:58,007
Import takes the whole box to your table.

32
00:01:58,033 --> 00:02:01,883
You still say which tool you want, as in
math dot ceil.

33
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:07,513
Where the comparison stops working: Python
runs the whole module file when it imports

34
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:07,711
it.

35
00:02:07,731 --> 00:02:10,807
So a print at the bottom of that file runs
too.

36
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:12,172
Quick check.

37
00:02:12,192 --> 00:02:15,133
After from math import ceil, which call
works?

38
00:02:26,513 --> 00:02:26,634
C.

39
00:02:26,654 --> 00:02:27,762
Ceil on its own.

40
00:02:27,782 --> 00:02:32,274
From import brings in only that one
function, not the name math.

41
00:02:32,887 --> 00:02:33,975
Take a breather.

42
00:02:33,995 --> 00:02:37,091
Next, Asha imports her first standard
module.

43
00:02:41,817 --> 00:02:42,378
Step 1.

44
00:02:42,398 --> 00:02:44,955
Line 2 imports the math module.

45
00:02:44,975 --> 00:02:50,607
Lines 4 and 5: Asha needs 40 biscuits, and
they come in boxes of 12.

46
00:02:50,633 --> 00:02:51,816
Line 6 divides.

47
00:02:51,836 --> 00:02:57,271
Line 7 uses math dot ceil, which rounds up
to the next whole number.

48
00:02:57,291 --> 00:03:00,960
Line 8 uses math dot floor, which rounds
down.

49
00:03:12,313 --> 00:03:13,072
4 and 3.

50
00:03:13,092 --> 00:03:21,153
40 divided by 12 is 3.33, so ceil rounds
up to 4 boxes, and floor rounds down to 3.

51
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:24,765
Line 9 prints the square root of 49: 7.0.

52
00:03:24,785 --> 00:03:29,837
The dot means: the function that lives in
the math module.

53
00:03:30,453 --> 00:03:30,929
Step 2.

54
00:03:30,949 --> 00:03:34,685
Lines 2 to 4 import in the three ways from
the table.

55
00:03:34,705 --> 00:03:38,653
Lines 6 to 8 call the same function in
those three ways.

56
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:40,063
All three print 3.

57
00:03:40,083 --> 00:03:45,133
In your own programs, pick one style, so
readers are not confused.

58
00:03:45,717 --> 00:03:46,206
Step 3.

59
00:03:46,226 --> 00:03:50,648
Asha moves her tools into their own file,
cafe tools dot p y.

60
00:03:50,668 --> 00:03:54,581
The name of a module is its file name
without dot p y.

61
00:03:54,601 --> 00:03:59,897
Line 2 is a docstring for the whole
module: it says what the file is for.

62
00:03:59,933 --> 00:04:03,793
It holds with tax and money, each with a
docstring.

63
00:04:03,833 --> 00:04:07,244
Lines 12 to 15 are test lines, under a
special if.

64
00:04:07,264 --> 00:04:12,940
Python gives every file a hidden variable
called name, with two underscores on each

65
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:13,283
side.

66
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:17,728
When you run the file itself, that
variable holds the text main.

67
00:04:17,748 --> 00:04:22,833
When another program imports the file, it
holds the module's name instead.

68
00:04:22,867 --> 00:04:27,919
Asha runs the module itself, so the test
lines run: two times True.

69
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:34,520
Now a second program, in the same folder,
imports the module on line 2.

70
00:04:34,567 --> 00:04:38,421
Line 4 calls cafe tools dot with tax, with
4.0.

71
00:04:38,441 --> 00:04:41,306
Line 6 shows the result with money.

72
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:53,947
4.4, then 4.40.

73
00:04:53,967 --> 00:04:55,492
And no test lines.

74
00:04:55,512 --> 00:05:01,929
The module was imported, so its name was
not main, and the if skipped them.

75
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:08,220
A third program uses from import, so it
calls with tax and money on their own.

76
00:05:08,267 --> 00:05:13,383
The loop prints three prices with tax:
3.85, 4.40 and 3.03.

77
00:05:13,403 --> 00:05:17,127
One copy of with tax, used by two
programs.

78
00:05:17,167 --> 00:05:21,953
Python looks for cafe tools in the folder
of the program you run.

79
00:05:21,973 --> 00:05:26,094
So keep your module next to the programs
that import it.

80
00:05:26,687 --> 00:05:27,378
Quick check.

81
00:05:27,398 --> 00:05:29,929
When do the lines under the main guard
run?

82
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:41,403
B.

83
00:05:41,423 --> 00:05:43,904
Only when you run that file itself.

84
00:05:43,924 --> 00:05:46,906
When it is imported, the guard skips them.

85
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:48,139
Breather.

86
00:05:48,159 --> 00:05:49,631
Relax your shoulders.

87
00:05:49,651 --> 00:05:54,604
Next, two more standard modules: one for
time, and one for file names.

88
00:05:59,350 --> 00:05:59,889
Step 4.

89
00:05:59,909 --> 00:06:02,765
The datetime module works with time.

90
00:06:02,785 --> 00:06:05,402
A time delta is a length of time.

91
00:06:05,422 --> 00:06:06,840
Line 2 imports it.

92
00:06:06,867 --> 00:06:10,872
Line 4: the cafe opens 8 hours after
midnight.

93
00:06:10,892 --> 00:06:15,160
Line 5: it stays open for 9 hours and 30
minutes.

94
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:17,611
Line 6 adds the two lengths.

95
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:31,962
At half past five in the afternoon.

96
00:06:31,982 --> 00:06:37,708
Python prints a time delta as hours,
minutes and seconds: 17:30:00.

97
00:06:37,728 --> 00:06:41,310
It carries the minutes into hours for you.

98
00:06:41,333 --> 00:06:45,530
Line 8: total seconds gives the length in
seconds.

99
00:06:45,550 --> 00:06:48,650
Divided by 60, that is 570.0 minutes.

100
00:06:49,253 --> 00:06:49,722
Step 5.

101
00:06:49,742 --> 00:06:53,423
Pathlib builds file names, without
touching any file.

102
00:06:53,443 --> 00:06:57,473
A path is the address of a file: its
folders and its name.

103
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,340
Line 4 joins a folder and a file name.

104
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:03,350
Here, the forward slash joins the parts.

105
00:07:03,370 --> 00:07:04,780
It does not divide.

106
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:06,791
Name is the whole file name.

107
00:07:06,811 --> 00:07:09,377
Stem is the name without its ending.

108
00:07:09,397 --> 00:07:10,885
Suffix is the ending.

109
00:07:22,247 --> 00:07:24,347
Monday, then dot t x t.

110
00:07:24,367 --> 00:07:28,607
Line 5 printed the whole name, monday dot
t x t.

111
00:07:28,633 --> 00:07:31,431
Windows writes paths with backslashes.

112
00:07:31,451 --> 00:07:36,843
So line 8, as posix, shows the path with
forward slashes on every system.

113
00:07:37,453 --> 00:07:38,385
Quick check.

114
00:07:38,405 --> 00:07:41,558
A path joins menus and winter dot t x t.

115
00:07:41,578 --> 00:07:43,065
What is its suffix?

116
00:07:54,447 --> 00:07:54,594
D.

117
00:07:54,614 --> 00:07:55,432
Dot t x t.

118
00:07:55,452 --> 00:07:57,526
The suffix is the ending.

119
00:07:57,546 --> 00:07:59,621
Winter would be the stem.

120
00:08:00,217 --> 00:08:01,573
Back to the mission.

121
00:08:01,593 --> 00:08:07,146
Lines 2 to 5 put every import at the top,
as the style guide asks: three standard

122
00:08:07,166 --> 00:08:08,867
modules, then Asha's own.

123
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:14,349
Line 8 adds tax to each sale with with
tax, in a comprehension from Chapter 14,

124
00:08:14,369 --> 00:08:16,080
and sum adds the results.

125
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:21,850
Line 9 rounds up the biscuit boxes, line
10 adds the opening hours, and line 11

126
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:24,042
builds the report's file name.

127
00:08:24,062 --> 00:08:26,087
It does not save a file yet.

128
00:08:37,447 --> 00:08:38,610
Total: 11.28.

129
00:08:38,630 --> 00:08:44,527
That is 3.85, plus 4.40, plus 3.03: the
three prices from step 3.

130
00:08:44,567 --> 00:08:48,802
Then 4 boxes, closing at 17:30, and the
file name.

131
00:08:48,822 --> 00:08:51,270
No copy of with tax anywhere.

132
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:52,717
Mission complete!

133
00:08:52,767 --> 00:08:58,065
Now Asha wants one more tool for her
module: how many boxes to order.

134
00:08:58,085 --> 00:09:02,381
She builds it with an AI assistant, one
stage at a time.

135
00:09:02,983 --> 00:09:08,823
This chapter's AI skill: ask an AI which
standard module fits a task.

136
00:09:08,867 --> 00:09:14,400
Then find that function in the official
documentation, at docs dot python dot org,

137
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:16,703
and run it on your own test cases.

138
00:09:16,723 --> 00:09:18,667
And never paste private data.

139
00:09:19,287 --> 00:09:20,286
Stage 1: plan.

140
00:09:20,306 --> 00:09:24,220
Asha describes the task: boxes of 12,
always round up.

141
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:29,537
She asks for the exact module and
function, and says she will check them.

142
00:09:29,567 --> 00:09:34,487
The AI names the math module and math dot
ceil, with an example.

143
00:09:34,533 --> 00:09:39,729
Asha opens the math page of the official
documentation and finds ceil, with the

144
00:09:39,749 --> 00:09:40,587
same meaning.

145
00:09:41,187 --> 00:09:42,298
Stage 2: write.

146
00:09:42,318 --> 00:09:48,334
Before using the module, Asha writes her
own version: double slash, then plus 1.

147
00:09:48,354 --> 00:09:50,447
For 40 biscuits, it gives 4.

148
00:09:50,467 --> 00:09:55,338
She asks the AI whether her version always
matches a ceil version.

149
00:09:55,358 --> 00:09:57,117
The AI says yes, always.

150
00:09:57,720 --> 00:10:03,007
Asha tests two numbers: 40, and 24, which
divides exactly.

151
00:10:03,027 --> 00:10:05,020
Line 5 is her version.

152
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:06,575
Line 6 uses ceil.

153
00:10:17,947 --> 00:10:18,158
No.

154
00:10:18,178 --> 00:10:23,020
For 24 biscuits, her version orders 3
boxes, and ceil orders 2.

155
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:27,187
Her version adds a box even when nothing
is left over.

156
00:10:27,233 --> 00:10:28,729
The AI missed her bug.

157
00:10:28,749 --> 00:10:31,415
A number that divides exactly found it.

158
00:10:31,435 --> 00:10:36,857
So she asks the AI to write the module's
version, and she will test it the same

159
00:10:36,877 --> 00:10:37,132
way.

160
00:10:37,753 --> 00:10:39,114
Stage 3: generate.

161
00:10:39,134 --> 00:10:44,483
Asha asks for boxes needed, with a
docstring, and asks exactly what it

162
00:10:44,503 --> 00:10:46,323
returns for 40 biscuits.

163
00:10:46,367 --> 00:10:50,343
The AI writes the function, and says it
returns 4.

164
00:10:50,953 --> 00:10:53,069
Here is the AI's function.

165
00:10:53,089 --> 00:10:57,093
Line 4 divides, then calls round, from
Chapter 2.

166
00:10:57,113 --> 00:10:59,392
Line 6 asks for 40 biscuits.

167
00:11:10,747 --> 00:11:10,983
No.

168
00:11:11,003 --> 00:11:12,007
It prints 3.

169
00:11:12,027 --> 00:11:16,956
Round goes to the nearest whole number, so
3.33 becomes 3.

170
00:11:16,976 --> 00:11:19,602
Asha would be 4 biscuits short.

171
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:21,266
Stage 4: test.

172
00:11:21,286 --> 00:11:27,586
Asha writes four cases with the result she
expects: a remainder, an exact division,

173
00:11:27,606 --> 00:11:29,261
one biscuit, and zero.

174
00:11:29,281 --> 00:11:32,230
She asks the AI if any case is missing.

175
00:11:32,267 --> 00:11:36,942
The AI says the set is good, and tells her
to run it on every version.

176
00:11:37,553 --> 00:11:42,771
Line 5 holds the cases, in a dictionary:
biscuits, and the expected boxes.

177
00:11:42,791 --> 00:11:45,743
The loop prints pass or FAIL for each one.

178
00:11:45,767 --> 00:11:48,653
On the AI's version, 40 and 1 fail.

179
00:11:48,673 --> 00:11:52,556
Round never rounds 1 twelfth up to a whole
box.

180
00:11:53,153 --> 00:11:54,259
Stage 5: debug.

181
00:11:54,279 --> 00:11:58,985
Asha changes round to math dot ceil, runs
it, and Python stops.

182
00:11:59,005 --> 00:12:04,162
She pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

183
00:12:04,753 --> 00:12:09,550
The AI names line 4: the file uses math,
but never imports it.

184
00:12:09,570 --> 00:12:13,512
The error even asks: did you forget to
import math?

185
00:12:13,532 --> 00:12:16,309
The fix: add import math at the top.

186
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:20,680
Line 2 imports math, and line 5 uses ceil.

187
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:23,110
Asha runs every case again.

188
00:12:23,133 --> 00:12:26,802
All four pass, including the exact
division and zero.

189
00:12:27,420 --> 00:12:28,695
Stage 6: improve.

190
00:12:28,715 --> 00:12:34,789
Asha asks for one improvement, and for the
documentation page of any function it

191
00:12:34,809 --> 00:12:35,170
uses.

192
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:37,812
The AI suggests math dot ceil div.

193
00:12:37,832 --> 00:12:43,772
It divides and rounds up in one step, and
the AI says it is on the math page.

194
00:12:44,387 --> 00:12:47,262
Before she changes anything, Asha checks.

195
00:12:47,282 --> 00:12:51,428
She searches the math page for ceil div,
and runs one line.

196
00:13:02,780 --> 00:13:02,978
No.

197
00:13:02,998 --> 00:13:06,966
Attribute Error: module math has no
attribute ceil div.

198
00:13:06,986 --> 00:13:10,374
Here, attribute means a name inside the
module.

199
00:13:10,394 --> 00:13:13,347
Ceil div was not on the math page either.

200
00:13:13,367 --> 00:13:15,450
That function does not exist.

201
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:18,982
The AI made it up, and named a real page
for it.

202
00:13:19,587 --> 00:13:23,902
Asha keeps math dot ceil, and runs her
four cases once more.

203
00:13:23,922 --> 00:13:25,348
All four still pass.

204
00:13:25,953 --> 00:13:26,752
Quick check.

205
00:13:26,772 --> 00:13:29,685
An AI names a function you have never
seen.

206
00:13:29,705 --> 00:13:31,117
What do you do first?

207
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:42,602
A.

208
00:13:42,622 --> 00:13:46,215
Find it in the official documentation,
then run it.

209
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:47,440
Breather.

210
00:13:47,460 --> 00:13:53,124
Asha caught three AI problems: a false
promise about her own code, round instead

211
00:13:53,144 --> 00:13:56,251
of ceil, and a function that does not
exist.

212
00:14:00,983 --> 00:14:01,943
Now you try.

213
00:14:01,963 --> 00:14:04,312
Line 2 imports Asha's module.

214
00:14:04,332 --> 00:14:06,190
Compare it with step 3.

215
00:14:17,547 --> 00:14:20,114
The name is cafe tool, without the s.

216
00:14:20,134 --> 00:14:25,918
Python looks for a file with exactly that
name, finds none, and stops with a Module

217
00:14:25,938 --> 00:14:27,037
Not Found Error.

218
00:14:27,067 --> 00:14:30,611
Some Python versions also suggest the
right name.

219
00:14:30,631 --> 00:14:32,939
The fix: spell it like the file.

220
00:14:33,553 --> 00:14:38,088
Mistake 2 is the quick check from the idea
section, in reverse.

221
00:14:38,108 --> 00:14:41,702
After import math, the full name is math
dot ceil.

222
00:14:41,722 --> 00:14:43,943
Ceil on its own is not defined.

223
00:14:43,967 --> 00:14:49,068
The fix: write math dot ceil, or import it
with from math import ceil.

224
00:14:49,687 --> 00:14:50,448
Mistake 3.

225
00:14:50,468 --> 00:14:55,686
Asha saved a test file called random dot p
y, next to this program.

226
00:14:55,706 --> 00:15:00,377
Now import random finds her file first,
not Python's module.

227
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:03,925
The first output line comes from her test
file.

228
00:15:03,945 --> 00:15:09,280
Then her file has no rand int, so Python
stops with an Attribute Error.

229
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:13,965
Python 3.13 and newer add advice: consider
renaming the file.

230
00:15:13,985 --> 00:15:19,188
On your own computer, that message shows
the full path of your file.

231
00:15:19,208 --> 00:15:23,413
The fix: rename it, for example to random
test dot p y.

232
00:15:24,020 --> 00:15:24,734
Mistake 4.

233
00:15:24,754 --> 00:15:29,868
This module has a test line at the bottom,
line 5, with no main guard.

234
00:15:29,888 --> 00:15:32,802
Run on its own, it prints its test line.

235
00:15:33,420 --> 00:15:37,837
Another program imports money from that
module, and prints the tea price.

236
00:15:49,213 --> 00:15:52,430
Two lines: the module's test line, then
the tea.

237
00:15:52,450 --> 00:15:58,161
Importing runs the whole file, so the test
line appears in every program that imports

238
00:15:58,181 --> 00:15:58,363
it.

239
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:01,826
There is no error, which makes it easy to
miss.

240
00:16:01,846 --> 00:16:05,125
The fix: put test lines under the main
guard.

241
00:16:05,720 --> 00:16:06,266
Breather.

242
00:16:06,286 --> 00:16:08,716
Two questions to think about come next.

243
00:16:13,450 --> 00:16:13,812
First.

244
00:16:13,832 --> 00:16:17,947
Asha wants to pick a random winner from a
list of customer names.

245
00:16:17,967 --> 00:16:20,555
Which function of the random module fits?

246
00:16:31,913 --> 00:16:37,120
Ask an AI, then find the function in the
official documentation, and run it.

247
00:16:37,720 --> 00:16:38,233
Second.

248
00:16:38,253 --> 00:16:41,661
Two folders of programs both need cafe
tools.

249
00:16:41,681 --> 00:16:46,459
Asha copies it into both folders, then
fixes a bug in one copy.

250
00:16:46,479 --> 00:16:47,449
What happens?

251
00:16:58,813 --> 00:17:02,131
Think back to the three copies at the
start of this chapter.

252
00:17:02,717 --> 00:17:03,432
Let's recap.

253
00:17:03,452 --> 00:17:08,088
A module is a Python file whose functions
other programs can import, and the

254
00:17:08,108 --> 00:17:11,887
standard library is the set of modules
that comes with Python.

255
00:17:11,933 --> 00:17:14,311
There are three ways to import.

256
00:17:14,331 --> 00:17:20,343
Importing runs the whole file once, and
the main guard keeps test lines quiet.

257
00:17:20,367 --> 00:17:24,458
Ceil rounds up, time delta adds times, and
Path reads file names.

258
00:17:24,478 --> 00:17:29,202
And when an AI names a function, find it
in the documentation, then run it.

259
00:17:29,820 --> 00:17:30,951
One last check.

260
00:17:30,971 --> 00:17:34,098
After import math as m, which call works?

261
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:45,620
C.

262
00:17:45,640 --> 00:17:47,055
M dot square root.

263
00:17:47,075 --> 00:17:50,964
The alias m is the only name that import
creates.

264
00:17:51,587 --> 00:17:52,981
Mission complete!

265
00:17:53,001 --> 00:17:56,474
You earned 100 XP and the Librarian badge.

266
00:17:58,717 --> 00:18:02,090
Now it is your turn: eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

267
00:18:02,110 --> 00:18:07,648
Three warm-up tasks at 10 XP, and three
apply tasks at 20, including your own

268
00:18:07,668 --> 00:18:08,587
price module.

269
00:18:08,633 --> 00:18:14,975
And two stretch tasks at 40, including an
AI's cake planner that calls a function

270
00:18:14,995 --> 00:18:17,331
the math module does not have.

271
00:18:17,351 --> 00:18:20,629
Try each one before you open the solution.

272
00:18:21,217 --> 00:18:24,542
Asha's module checks itself with lines
that print True.

273
00:18:24,562 --> 00:18:28,009
But if a change makes one print False,
nothing stops her.

274
00:18:28,029 --> 00:18:31,477
And every program that imports the module
breaks with it.

275
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:37,280
In Chapter 19, you will turn these checks
into real tests, with assert and pytest.

276
00:18:37,300 --> 00:18:39,082
Do the warm-up tasks now.

277
00:18:39,102 --> 00:18:41,100
They take about ten minutes.

278
00:18:41,133 --> 00:18:43,016
See you in Chapter 19.
